/**
@page applications_exahype2_ExaGRyPE ExaGRyPE

ExaGRyPE is the ExaHyPE 2 application for numerical relativity experiments
based on the first-order CCZ4 formulation of Einstein's equations. It
provides the gauge-wave application with finite-volume, ADER-DG and Runge-Kutta DG solvers.
The solver families call the same CCZ4 operators.

The driver in @c gauge-wave accepts @c --solver with @c fv, @c aderdg or @c rkdg.
The option @c --degrees-of-freedom sets the number of nodes per axis, and
@c --grid-levels sets a regular grid's refinement level.
This command generates and builds a third-order Runge-Kutta scheme with cubic spatial polynomials:
@code
python3 gauge-wave.py --solver rkdg --degrees-of-freedom 4 --grid-levels 2
@endcode
The environment variable @c PEANO_BUILD_DIR selects the CMake library build.

The unknowns are grouped as
@code
{
  "gamma_tilde": exahype2.SymmetricMatrix(3),
  "A_tilde": exahype2.SymmetricMatrix(3),
  "theta": 1,
  "gamma_hat": 3,
  "alpha": 1,
  "beta": 3,
  "b": 3,
  "grad_alpha": 3,
  "grad_beta": (3, 3),
  "grad_gamma_tilde": (3, exahype2.SymmetricMatrix(3)),
  "K": 1,
  "phi": 1,
  "grad_phi": 3,
}
@endcode

The entries represent:
- @f$\tilde{\gamma}_{ij}@f$: conformal metric, stored as
  @f$(xx,yy,zz,xy,xz,yz)@f$.
- @f$\tilde{A}_{ij}@f$: traceless extrinsic curvature, stored as
  @f$(xx,yy,zz,xy,xz,yz)@f$.
- @f$\Theta@f$: temporal component of the Z4 vector.
- @f$\hat{\Gamma}^i@f$: conformal connection functions, the conformal Christoffel symbols traced
  with the conformal metric, together with the spatial Z4 contribution, stored as @f$(x,y,z)@f$.
- @f$\alpha@f$: lapse.
- @f$\beta^i@f$: shift vector, stored as @f$(x,y,z)@f$.
- @f$b^i@f$: gamma-driver auxiliary vector, stored as @f$(x,y,z)@f$.
- `grad_alpha`: the first derivatives of the lapse,
  @f$A_i = \partial_i \alpha@f$, stored as @f$(x,y,z)@f$.
- `grad_beta`: the first derivatives of the shift,
  @f$B_{ij} = \partial_i \beta^j@f$, stored as
  @f$(xx,xy,xz,yx,yy,yz,zx,zy,zz)@f$.
- `grad_gamma_tilde`: half the first derivatives of the conformal metric,
  @f$D_{kij} = \tfrac{1}{2} \partial_k \tilde{\gamma}_{ij}@f$, grouped by
  derivative direction:
  @f$x:(xx,yy,zz,xy,xz,yz)@f$,
  @f$y:(xx,yy,zz,xy,xz,yz)@f$,
  @f$z:(xx,yy,zz,xy,xz,yz)@f$.
- @f$K@f$: trace of the extrinsic curvature.
- @f$\phi@f$: conformal factor
  @f$\phi = (\det \gamma_{ij})^{-1/6}@f$. The conformal metric
  @f$\tilde{\gamma}_{ij} = \phi^2 \gamma_{ij}@f$ then has unit determinant.
- `grad_phi`: the first derivatives of the conformal factor,
  @f$P_i = \partial_i \phi@f$, stored as @f$(x,y,z)@f$.

In terms of state-vector indices, the layout is
@code
[0]  gamma_tilde_xx   [1]  gamma_tilde_yy   [2]  gamma_tilde_zz
[3]  gamma_tilde_xy   [4]  gamma_tilde_xz   [5]  gamma_tilde_yz
[6]  A_tilde_xx       [7]  A_tilde_yy       [8]  A_tilde_zz
[9]  A_tilde_xy       [10] A_tilde_xz       [11] A_tilde_yz
[12] theta
[13] gamma_hat_x      [14] gamma_hat_y      [15] gamma_hat_z
[16] alpha
[17] beta_x           [18] beta_y           [19] beta_z
[20] b_x              [21] b_y              [22] b_z
[23] A_x              [24] A_y              [25] A_z
[26] B_xx             [27] B_xy             [28] B_xz
[29] B_yx             [30] B_yy             [31] B_yz
[32] B_zx             [33] B_zy             [34] B_zz
[35] D_x_xx           [36] D_x_yy           [37] D_x_zz
[38] D_x_xy           [39] D_x_xz           [40] D_x_yz
[41] D_y_xx           [42] D_y_yy           [43] D_y_zz
[44] D_y_xy           [45] D_y_xz           [46] D_y_yz
[47] D_z_xx           [48] D_z_yy           [49] D_z_zz
[50] D_z_xy           [51] D_z_xz           [52] D_z_yz
[53] K
[54] phi
[55] P_x              [56] P_y              [57] P_z
@endcode
*/
